<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Darłowo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rügenwalde</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Darłowo (German: Rügenwalde) is a small town on the Baltic coast in the West Pomeranian Voivodeship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, near the mouth of the Wieprza. Its castle was a residence of the Pomeranian dukes, and from 1449 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-of-pomerania">Eric of Pomerania</a>, deposed from his Scandinavian thrones, lived and ruled there until his death in 1459. He is buried in the town's Church of St Mary.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/darlowo.jpg" alt="Darłowo" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vit · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dar%C5%82owo_radnice_a_kostel_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Darłowo is a seaside town on the Slovincian Coast of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>, in Sławno County. By the 11th century a fortress named Dirlow existed where the River Wieprza enters the Baltic, and from it a district belonging to the castellany of Sławno was administered. A town was later founded in the district, though not at the fortress itself. The original medieval outline of the Old Town, with its main square in the middle, has been preserved.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town was probably founded in 1270 by Vitslav II of the Danish Principality of Rügen, and is first mentioned in a document of 5 February 1271. It was granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> law on 21 May 1312 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> margraves, passed to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> in 1347, and began building its castle in 1352. It became a full member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> in 1412 and had a trade fleet larger than those of the surrounding towns, trading with Lübeck and sending ships as far as Normandy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-of-pomerania">Eric of Pomerania</a>, later king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, was born in the town in 1382. After losing his thrones he returned to his birthplace and expanded his duchy, modernising and extending the castle between 1449 and 1459. He was buried in St Mary's Church after his death in 1459. From 1569 to 1622 Darłowo was capital of a small duchy of its own. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/further-pomerania">Farther Pomerania</a> with Rügenwalde into its province, and the town entered a period of economic stagnation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In 1871 the town became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>, and a railway reached it in 1878. In 1935 a German Army firing range for heavy guns was built nearby. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the Potsdam Conference assigned the town to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, its German population was expelled, and it was repopulated in 1946-47 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemkos">Lemkos</a>. The Gothic castle, on a plan resembling a square with a tower 24 metres high, now serves as a museum, and the Old Town and its beaches draw holidaymakers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Darłowo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>One Griffin prince, Eric of Pomerania, a great-nephew of Margaret I of Denmark, was crowned king of Denmark, Norway and Sweden in 1397 at the creation of the Kalmar Union; deposed in the late 1430s, he ended his life ruling a small Pomeranian duchy at <b>Darłowo</b> (Rügenwalde).</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The duchy covered Further Pomerania, a land of farms, forests and small towns such as Stolp and <b>Rügenwalde</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-pomerania-stolp">Duchy of Pomerania-Stolp</a></li></ul></section>
